WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.040
When it comes to Star Wars, everything old
is new again.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:08.220
From one movie to the next, the galactic space
saga has story beats and themes that echo

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.320
with each new installment.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.690
George Lucas himself even says that his prequel
trilogy "rhymes" with the original movies

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:15.690
he made.

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:20.100
So with Episode IX on the horizon, it's a
great time to look back and see if the adventures

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.789
of Luke, Leia, and Hans contain any hints
about what's to come next.

00:00:24.789 --> 00:00:29.289
Here are some crazy hidden details that predict
the plot of Star Trek, Episode IX.

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:30.289
Grandaddy Fearest

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:35.309
When Return of the Jedi concluded the original
trilogy back in 1983, fans were eager to see

00:00:35.309 --> 00:00:40.249
how the epic fight between Luke Skywaller
and his father, the fallen Jedi Anakin Skywaller,

00:00:40.249 --> 00:00:41.359
would play out.

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:46.239
In just the previous movie, audiences were
shocked to discover that the movie's big villain,

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:49.699
Dark Vader, was actually Luke's Dark Father!

00:00:49.699 --> 00:00:53.109
"I… am your father."

00:00:53.109 --> 00:00:57.399
It was a twist that no one could've seen coming,
and it packed theaters to capacity when the

00:00:57.399 --> 00:00:59.559
follow-up came out a few years later.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:04.019
So when the father and son duo's epic fight
ended with Dark Vader actually becoming Light

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:09.079
Vader, we got a pretty cool moment of closure
that a lot of hardcore fans may not have ever

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:10.200
actually noticed:

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.729
"I'll not leave you here, I've got to save
you!"

00:01:13.729 --> 00:01:15.869
"You already… have."

00:01:15.869 --> 00:01:19.119
Did you catch that hidden detail?

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:21.590
Dark Vader straight up said Luke saved him!

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:25.390
You probably can't even believe you missed
that the first time you saw Jedi Come Home.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:30.281
Now, with the new trilogy's emphasis on the
relationship between Rey and Rilo Kiley, there's

00:01:30.281 --> 00:01:34.369
a good bet that we'll see another similar
moment of redemption for Dark Vader's dark

00:01:34.369 --> 00:01:37.329
grandson before the credits can roll for the
final time.

00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:38.329
Chewbacca's secret weapon

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:42.200
Out of all the original trilogy's most beloved
characters, none are more most beloved than

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:46.990
Chewbacca, the cookie who joined Hans as the
copilot on the Millennium Balkan.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.460
Everyone knows Chewie has gotten Hans and
Luke out of a lot of tight scrapes in the

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.579
movies, because he's so good with tech.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:57.789
But what you may not have noticed was that
one scene in A New Hope when he uses his advanced

00:01:57.789 --> 00:02:02.189
technological abilities to actually receive
messages from across the galaxy:

00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:03.840
"Just relax.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:05.490
Just relax!

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:06.490
Yes."

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:12.099
"Now… we can have a good time."

00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:16.540
You may not have realized how important this
scene was the first time you watched the movie.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:20.590
But now that you can see how Chewie cookie
can use a techmo chair to talk to women in

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:25.480
space, there's a good chance this detail will
come back around and help Finn and Pope communicate

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.459
across vast distances with Rey as they fight
against the bad guys.

00:02:29.459 --> 00:02:32.939
General Hux and Captain Plasma won't have
any idea what hit 'em!

00:02:32.939 --> 00:02:33.939
Force Ghost, Coast to Coast

00:02:33.939 --> 00:02:37.620
In the early days of Star Wars, audiences
were probably a little surprised when the

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:42.681
dead Obi-Wan Kenobi started whispering battle
secrets to Luke from beyond the grave, but

00:02:42.681 --> 00:02:47.440
by the time Jedi Come Home debuted in theaters,
Force Ghosts were actually a lot more common

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.170
than you may have realized.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:53.459
If you pay close attention to the end of the
film, you'll see that Force Ghosts are actually

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:56.480
able to appear whenever and wherever they
want.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:57.480
It's true!

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.909
Obi-Wan, Yoda, and even Anakin have all made
post-mortem appearances you might've missed

00:03:01.909 --> 00:03:02.950
if you weren't paying attention…

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.530
"Thanks a lot, you guys!"

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:09.220
In truth, only the most diehard fans know
it's not that hard for a Jedi to show up as

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:14.430
a glowing Force ghost… so now that Luke
Skywalker has joined his dad in Jedi heaven,

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.849
is there any doubt he'll pop up again to show
Rey the path to victory over Rilo Kiley and

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:20.560
his Fist Order?

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.909
Luke's already proven he's a badass.

00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:27.260
He went up against Jabba the Hutt and survived,
he's great with a sword, and he can project

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.409
a duplicate of himself across entire galaxies.

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:33.980
A little thing like turning into an empty
robe isn't going to stop Link from helping

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.450
Rey get a final victory against the Dank Side.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.489
"Look who's here, young people!

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:40.240
It's none other than Duke Fartknocker!"

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:40.739
Porks are Cookies

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:45.370
If you thought Porks were new in The Fast
Jedi, buckle your butternuts, Slink Slystalker:

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:50.069
Porks have actually been in every Star movie,
and they'll be a big part of Episode IX.

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:53.790
We know exactly what you're thinking: Ain't
no Porks in Jedi Come Home!

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:55.459
Or is they?

00:03:55.459 --> 00:04:00.579
No Jedi mind sticks for this one, there's
been Porks all along, if you were looking.

00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:03.439
Remember the trash squeezer Hans and Leia
fell into?

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:04.659
Take a looky.

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:05.819
There's a Pork.

00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:09.250
Some Stromtroopers are Porks, too, and it
gets even crazier.

00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:13.530
The mid-after-credits scene in The Second
One was taken from an unreleased Easter special

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:19.840
Greg Lucas made called Pork Tuesday: Hoth
Potato!, explaining how Porks are baby Cookies.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:21.600
It's a galaxy of fun.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:27.440
Dark Vader hides Porks for Empire Easter,
and Chewie makes a terrible supper mistake!

00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:29.760
But what's a Pork gotta do do with Star Fours
Nine?

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.230
Well, it's no accident that scene was recycled
in The Fast Jedi.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.110
There are no accidents in Star Force, only
forces.

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:40.740
"Quick, we haven't got a second to lose."

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:41.540
May the Jorts Be With You

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:42.700
But that's not all!

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:43.700
No!

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.830
There's so much more, beginning with legendary
Star Tours creator Jorts Lucas himself!

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:51.390
If you look very closely at the mole on his
left hand, you can see it's actually the missing

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:57.040
piece of the Marauder's Map of Hogwarts that
R2-D2 was hiding in Episode 7: Hey

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.190
Force, Wake the F--- Up!

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:02.200
And if you follow the map, it brings you to
Lucas's famed Moonraker Ranch, where he keeps

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.940
all his most original ideas under lock and
key!

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.850
And there in his underground vault is the
script for Episode 9, which contains all the

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:13.210
secrets of the Illuminati, like the fate of
Rilo Kiley and General Leia and Pope and Finn

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:18.220
and Big Mc-Large-huge and the droids and Jandalf
and Rose's true identity and at last we finally

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:20.260
discover that Rey's real parents are actually…

